        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ff8c66f4-59e6-5e4f-bb3f-5999bc10936a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Energy Northwest, Washington Public Power Supply System (WPPSS)\n- Projects: WNP-1, WNP-2, WNP-3, WNP-4, WNP-5, Satsop Nuclear Power Plant\n- Places: Pacific Northwest\n\nFacts:\n- In 1957, Washington Public Power Supply System, later renamed Energy Northwest, was formed by Washington state law as a United States public power joint operating agency to produce at-cost power for Northwest utilities.\n- In 1968, Washington Public Power Supply System's original plan included WNP-1, WNP-2, and WNP-3.\n- In the early 1970s, Washington Public Power Supply System added WNP-4 as a twin to WNP-1 at the same site and added WNP-5 as a twin to WNP-3.\n- Washington Public Power Supply System began construction on five nuclear power plants to meet projected electricity demand in the Pacific Northwest.\n- WNP-3 and WNP-5 were collectively known as the Satsop Nuclear Power Plant.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "2eba3f70-f249-5b05-b000-a138c7422f4e",
        "text": "Page: WNP-3 and WNP-5. Sentence: Washington Nuclear Project Nos. 3 and 5, abbreviated as WNP-3 and WNP-5 (collectively known as the Satsop Nuclear Power Plant) were two of the five nuclear power plants on which construction was started by the Washington Public Power Supply System (WPPSS) in order to meet projected electricity demand in the Pacific Northwest.\nPage: Energy Northwest. Sentence: Energy Northwest (formerly Washington Public Power Supply System) is a United States public power joint operating agency formed by State law in 1957 to produce at-cost power for Northwest utilities.\nPage: WNP-1 and WNP-4. Sentence: WNP-1, WNP-2 and WNP-3 were part of the original 1968 plan, with WNP-4 (a twin to WNP-1 and located at the same site) and WNP-5 (a twin to WNP-3, in similar fashion) added in the early 1970s.",
